3
00:00:14.800 --> 00:00:24.160
[Rebecca] Today, we explore an extraordinary, unforgettable saga from our church history and reformation collection: the National Covenant of 1638.

4
00:00:24.160 --> 00:00:37.280
[Rebecca] In February 1638, thousands of Scots gathered in the snow at Greyfriars Kirkyard in Edinburgh to sign a massive sheepskin parchment defying King Charles I's imposition of the English Book of Common Prayer.

5
00:00:37.280 --> 00:00:49.120
[Rebecca] Some wept, others drew blood from their own arms to sign their names, binding the Scottish nation in an unyielding covenant to defend the crown rights of King Jesus above any earthly monarch.

6
00:00:49.120 --> 00:01:07.760
[Rebecca] Across centuries, human civilization has turned not merely on the clash of vast empires or the dry clauses of international treaties, but on moments of intense personal conviction, split-second decisions made in smoke-filled rooms, and ordinary individuals who found the courage to defy impossible odds.

7
00:01:07.760 --> 00:01:14.000
[Rebecca] Here is George with the full, dramatized story.

8
00:01:24.960 --> 00:01:29.280
[George] The conflict that unfolded was not a sudden, isolated eruption.

9
00:01:29.280 --> 00:01:40.000
[George] It was the inevitable culmination of deep ideological tensions, institutional fractures, and human grievances that had been smoldering beneath the surface of society for generations.

10
00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:54.640
[George] Across the towns and countryside, long-established authorities found themselves challenged by radical new currents of thought, relentless geopolitical rivalries, and the rising, unyielding demands of citizens determined to shape their own destinies.

11
00:01:54.640 --> 00:02:05.720
[George] At the very center of the drama captured in the parchment signed in blood at Greyfriars Kirkyard lay a fundamental, irreconcilable dispute over authority, conscience, and survival.

12
00:02:05.720 --> 00:02:08.840
[George] Who possessed the moral and legal right to govern?

13
00:02:08.840 --> 00:02:17.800
[George] Was power derived from ancient hereditary privilege and state coercion, or did it spring from the voluntary consent and sacred rights of the people?

14
00:02:17.800 --> 00:02:31.560
[George] Primary documents and eyewitness dispatches preserved in original National Covenant Manuscripts, 1638, National Records of Scotland and Edinburgh City Archives, reveal an atmosphere thick with foreboding.

15
00:02:31.560 --> 00:02:43.720
[George] In ministerial offices, royal councils, and secluded meeting houses, key decision-makers met late into the night, weighing the terrible risks of action against the creeping dishonour of surrender.

16
00:02:43.720 --> 00:02:51.640
[George] Rumours and counter rumours raced through crowded streets, ordinary families sensed that the peace of their homeland was coming to an end.

17
00:02:51.640 --> 00:03:01.640
[George] As formal diplomatic channels began to break down and legal appeals stalled in high courts, the protagonists recognized that the hour of passive patience had passed.

18
00:03:01.640 --> 00:03:06.760
[George] The stage was set for a historic collision that would permanently shatter the existing order.

19
00:03:06.760 --> 00:03:14.680
[George] As the crisis rapidly escalated toward an open breach, the true nature of the stakes became unmistakable to all observers.

20
00:03:14.680 --> 00:03:23.400
[George] Those who stepped forward to lead the charge in the National Covenant of 1638 were not two-dimensional caricatures or abstract historical symbols.

21
00:03:23.400 --> 00:03:32.760
[George] They were flesh and blood human beings wrestling with private agony, profound self-doubt, and the terrifying awareness of what failure would cost their families.

22
00:03:32.760 --> 00:03:35.240
